    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d793">

                <pb facs="0797" n="699" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">839.00 Revolutions/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-02-24T11:00:00-04:40">February 24, 1930—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>12. The Vice President has presented his resignation and the Dominican Government
                    promises legislative annullment of all amendments to the electoral law made
                    since 1924. With this information Cabot<anchor n="4" xml:id="d793-f1"/> has gone
                    to endeavor to prevent bloodshed between the revolutionists and the troops sent
                    out to stop them. The retirement of Alfonseca should restore to the Government
                    the firm allegiance of the Government’s leaders throughout the country. There is
                    no reliable news from Santiago which is almost certainly in the possession of
                    the revolutionists.</p>
                <closer>[<signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Curtis</hi></signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d793-f1" n="4" xml:id="d793-f1-content">
                <p>John Moors Cabot, Third Secretary of Legation.</p>
